rk is stdlib, I don't need to install anything. I'll build the REST API using `http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer` with a custom request handler, and `sqlite3` for storage.\n\nPlan:\n1. `app.py` (or a package structure) — the main application:\n   - `BookStore` class handling SQLite operations (create, list w/ author filter, get, update, delete)\n   - `BookRequestHandler` — routes:\n     - GET /health → 200 {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n     - POST /books → 201 with created book; 400 if title/author missing/invalid\n     - GET /books → 200 list; supports ?author= filter (case-insensitive substring or exact? I'll do case-insensitive partial match — actually, let's think. \"support ?author= filter\" — simplest sensible: filter books whose author contains the query string, case-insensitive. That's a reasonable interpretation. Or exact match. I'll do case-insensitive partial match as it's more useful, and document in README.)\n     - GET /books/{id} → 200 or 404\n     - PUT /books/{id} → 200 updated; 400 invalid; 404 not found\n     - DELETE /books/{id} → 204 (or 200); 404 if not found\n   - JSON parsing errors → 400\n   - Validation: title and author required (non-empty strings). year must be an integer if provided; isbn optional string. Let me add validation for year: if provided must be int (or int-like). Keep it simple but robust.\n\n2. SQLite: file-based DB `books.db` (path configurable via env var BOOKS_DB). Use a lock for thread safety since ThreadingHTTPServer is multithreaded. sqlite3 connections: create per-request connection or use check_same_thread=False with a lock. Simpler: open a connection per request with a context manager, or a single connection guarded by threading.Lock. I'll open a new connection per operation — that's clean and thread-safe by default. Use WAL maybe not needed. Keep simple: `sqlite3.connect(db_path)` per call with row_factory.\n\n3. Tests: `test_app.py` using pytest — spin up the server in a thread on port 0 (ephemeral), use `urllib.request` to