import closing\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer\nfrom urllib.parse import parse_qs, urlparse\n\nDEFAULT_DB_PATH = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\")\nDEFAULT_PORT = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"8000\"))\n\n_BOOK_ID_RE = re.compile(r\"^/books/(\\d+)$\")\n\n\nclass ValidationError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a request payload fails validation.\"\"\"\n```\n\netc. Let me write the whole thing in the write call.\n\nValidation of year — also maybe reject absurd values? No.\n\nFor update: `db.update(book_id, title, author, year, isbn)`.\n\nAlso: PUT requires full object; document.\n\nWrite test_app.py and README.md, requirements.txt.\n\nTest details — test_create_missing_title etc. parametrize over payloads.\n\nFor the author filter test: create \"Jane Austen\" and \"George Orwell\". GET /books?author=jane → 1 result (case-insensitive substring). GET /books?author=orwell → 1. Hmm \"jane\" matches \"Jane Austen\" case-insensitively. Good.\n\nAlso test filter with no match → empty list.\n\ntest_invalid_json: send raw non-JSON body. My http_request helper always JSON-encodes; add param `raw_body` to send arbitrary bytes. I'll add a `data` param variant:\n\n```python\ndef http_request(base, method, path, payload=None, raw_body=None):\n    data = raw_body if raw_body is not None else (json.dumps(payload).encode() if payload is not None else None)\n```\n\nThen test: http_request(base, \"POST\", \"/books\", raw_body=b\"not json\") → 400.\n\nAlso test empty body POST → 400.\n\ntest_year_validation: {\"title\": \"X\", \"author\": \"Y\", \"year\": \"1999\"} → 400 (string year). And {\"title\": \"X\", \"author\": \"Y\", \"year\": 1999.5} → 400.\n\nOK. Writing files now.\n\nOne more consideration on `_send_json` and 204 with HTTP/1.1 keep-alive: BaseHTTPRequestHandler.send_response includes Server/Date headers. For 204 we send no Content-Length — per RFC 7230, a 204 response has no body regardless; clients know that. urllib handles